    "created_at": "2015-12-21T16:08:44Z",
    "updated_at": "2015-12-21T16:08:44Z",
    "body": "FWIW I like the names. To your point, these APIs must operate on a tree, so there's always a question of \"before what?\" and \"after what?\". The same questions could be said of insertBefore from a DOM newbie. I use the following logic to figure it out.\n1. before = insertBefore, ergo after = \"insertAfter\"... i.e. a sibling operation.\n2. append = appendChild, ergo prepend = \"prependChild\" a child list operation.\n\n_if_ I was considering an alterative short name, I might be inclined towards \"next\" and \"previous\" due to their similarity to nextSibling and previousSibling--unfortunately those names are probably also confusing in their own way, not to mention that \"next\" runs into problems with iterators...\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
